<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Torre de Palma</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Torre de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palma">Palma</a> is the site of a large Roman villa near Monforte in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alentejo">Alentejo</a> region of Portugal, occupied from the 1st century CE into late antiquity. It is known for its 4th-century mosaics, among them the Mosaic of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muses">Muses</a> and a mosaic of named horses, now in the National Archaeological Museum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a>. The site also includes an early Christian basilica and baptistery.</p></div>
